{"type":"message","message":{"id":"gen-1775253715-tOE2MGAhP2ON9gJvBqtN","role":"assistant","created":1775253755,"content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the convertible foreign currencies data from both documents. Let me extract the annual data for CY 1963-1977:\n\nFrom the 1970 bulletin (page 94) - I can see data up to 1969\nFrom the 1978 bulletin (page 91) - I can see data from 1968-1977\n\nLooking at both sources, I need to extract the convertible foreign currencies (also called \"Convertible foreign currencies\" or \"Convertible foreign currencies 4/\")\n\nFrom the 1978 bulletin (more complete for the 1968-1977 period):\n- 1968: 3,528\n- 1969: 2,781 (7/ indicates a footnote, but the value is 2,781)\n- 1970: 629\n- 1971: 276 (8/ indicates footnote)\n- 1972: 241\n- 1973: 8\n- 1974: 5\n- 1975: 80\n- 1976: 320\n- 1977: 18\n\nFrom the 1970 bulletin, I can verify some of these:\n- 1963: 212\n- 1964: 432\n- 1965: 781\n- 1966: 1,321\n- 1967: 2,345\n\nSo for CY 1963-1977 inclusive:\n- 1963: 212\n- 1964: 432\n- 1965: 781\n- 1966: 1,321\n- 1967: 2,345\n- 1968: 3,528\n- 1969: 2,781\n- 1970: 629\n- 1971: 276\n- 1972: 241\n- 1973: 8\n- 1974: 5\n- 1975: 80\n- 1976: 320\n- 1977: 18\n\n\nI've confirmed the values are in millions of dollars. Now I'll proceed with calculating the Fisher-Pearson adjusted skewness, starting with computing the sample mean and standard deviation for this 15-year dataset.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_f4edd2828d1f5275","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"shell","arguments":{"command":"python3 << 'EOF'\nimport math\n\n# Data values (convertible foreign currencies in millions of dollars)\ndata = [212, 432, 781, 1321, 2345, 3528, 2781, 629, 276, 241, 8, 5, 80, 320, 18]\n\nn = len(data)\nprint(f\"n = {n}\")\n\n# Calculate sample mean\nmean = sum(data) / n\nprint(f\"Mean = {mean}\")\n\n# Calculate sample standard deviation (using n-1 denominator)\nvariance = sum((x - mean) ** 2 for x in data) / (n - 1)\nstd_dev = math.sqrt(variance)\nprint(f\"Sample Standard Deviation = {std_dev}\")\n\n# Calculate Fisher-Pearson adjusted skewness\n# g1 = (n / ((n-1)*(n-2))) * sum((xi - x̄)^3) / s^3\n# But the adjusted version is: G1 = ((n*(n-1))**0.5 / (n-2)) * (mom3 / s^3)\n# More commonly: Fisher-Pearson coefficient of skewness = g1 * sqrt(n*(n-1)) / (n-2)\n\n# First calculate the third moment about the mean\nmom3 = sum((x - mean) ** 3 for x in data) / n\nprint(f\"Third moment about mean (m3) = {mom3}\")\n\n# Calculate g1 (sample skewness)\ng1 = (n * mom3) / ((n - 1) * (n - 2) * (std_dev ** 3))\nprint(f\"g1 (sample skewness) = {g1}\")\n\n# Fisher-Pearson adjusted skewness\n# G1 = g1 * sqrt(n*(n-1)) / (n-2)\nG1 = g1 * math.sqrt(n * (n - 1)) / (n - 2)\nprint(f\"Fisher-Pearson adjusted skewness (G1) = {G1}\")\n\n# Let me also verify using a direct formula\n# The standardized third moment\nsum_cubed = sum(((x - mean) / std_dev) ** 3 for x in data)\nprint(f\"Sum of cubed z-scores = {sum_cubed}\")\n\n# Another way: using the formula with n\n# Fisher-Pearson: G1 = [n / ((n-1)(n-2))] * sum((xi - xbar)^3) / s^3 * sqrt((n-1)/n)\n# Wait, that's not right either\n\n# Let me recalculate with proper formula\n# The sample skewness (g1) is: n/(n-1)(n-2) * sum((xi-xbar)^3) / s^3\n# Then Fisher-Pearson is: g1 * sqrt(n*(n-1)) / (n-2)\n\n# Actually let's check the exact formula\n# Fisher-Pearson coefficient of skewness: \n# G1 = (sqrt(n*(n-1)) / (n-2)) * (1/n * sum((xi-mu)^3)) / s^3 \n# where s is sample standard deviation\n\n# Let me use the exact formula from statistics\nnumerator = sum((x - mean) ** 3 for x in data) / n\nG1_exact = (math.sqrt(n * (n - 1)) / (n - 2)) * (numerator / (std_dev ** 3))\nprint(f\"Exact Fisher-Pearson G1 = {G1_exact}\")\n\n# Round to 3 decimal places\nprint(f\"\\nRounded to 3 decimal places: {round(G1_exact, 3)}\")\nEOF"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"developer"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
